<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Picton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1758–1815</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Thomas Picton (1758–1815) was a Welsh-born British general who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> after its capture from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1797. His harsh rule, including the authorised torture of a young girl, Luisa Calderón, led to his trial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He later commanded a division under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> in the Peninsular War and was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-picton.jpg" alt="Thomas Picton" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Beechey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lieutenant-General_Sir_Thomas_Picton_A17581.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
